# DOM Reflow & Repaint

When you change something on a webpage, the browser has to figure out how the page should look again.

For example:

* Changing an element's size
* Moving an element
* Changing its color
* Adding or removing elements
* Updating text

The browser may need to recalculate the layout and redraw parts of the page.

Two important concepts are:

* **Reflow**
* **Repaint**

Understanding them helps you write more efficient JavaScript and build smoother websites.

---

# What Happens When a Browser Renders a Page?

A simplified rendering process looks like:

```text
HTML
 ↓
DOM
 ↓
CSS
 ↓
CSSOM
 ↓
Render Tree
 ↓
Layout
 ↓
Paint
 ↓
Composite
 ↓
Screen
```

The browser uses these steps to turn HTML and CSS into what you see on the screen.

---

# What is Reflow?

**Reflow** happens when the browser has to recalculate the layout of elements.

In other words:

> The browser needs to figure out where elements should be and how much space they should occupy again.

For example:

```javascript
element.style.width = "500px";
```

Changing the width can affect:

* The element itself
* Its children
* Its parent
* Nearby elements

So the browser may need to recalculate the layout.

---

# Example of Reflow

Consider:

```html
<div id="box">
    <p>Hello</p>
</div>
```

Now:

```javascript
const box = document.getElementById("box");

box.style.width = "500px";
```

The browser may need to recalculate:

```text
Box width
   ↓
Children layout
   ↓
Nearby elements
   ↓
Overall page layout
```

That recalculation is reflow.

---

# What Can Cause Reflow?

Common examples include changing:

```javascript
element.style.width = "500px";
element.style.height = "300px";
element.style.margin = "20px";
element.style.padding = "10px";
```

Also:

```javascript
element.style.display = "none";
```

or:

```javascript
element.style.display = "block";
```

Other operations that can cause layout recalculation include:

* Adding elements
* Removing elements
* Changing text
* Changing font size
* Changing element dimensions
* Changing positioning
* Reading certain layout properties after changes

---

# What is Repaint?

**Repaint** happens when the browser needs to redraw an element without necessarily recalculating its layout.

For example:

```javascript
element.style.color = "red";
```

The element's position and size haven't changed.

The browser mainly needs to redraw its appearance.

That is a repaint.

---

# Example of Repaint

```javascript
const heading = document.querySelector("h1");

heading.style.color = "red";
```

The browser doesn't normally need to calculate a completely new layout just because the text color changed.

Instead:

```text
Existing Layout
      ↓
Change color
      ↓
Redraw affected pixels
```

That's repaint.

---

# Reflow vs Repaint

| Reflow                              | Repaint                           |
| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Recalculates layout                 | Redraws appearance                |
| Can affect surrounding elements     | Usually affects visual appearance |
| Generally more expensive            | Generally less expensive          |
| Width/height changes can cause it   | Color changes can cause it        |
| Position/layout calculations happen | Pixels are redrawn                |

A reflow can also lead to repainting because after the layout changes, the browser may need to redraw the affected areas.

---

# Simple Example

Suppose we have:

```html
<div id="box"></div>
```

CSS:

```css
#box {
    width: 200px;
    height: 100px;
    background: blue;
}
```

Changing:

```javascript
box.style.width = "400px";
```

can cause:

```text
Reflow
 ↓
Layout changes
 ↓
Repaint
```

But changing:

```javascript
box.style.backgroundColor = "red";
```

may only require:

```text
Repaint
```

because the dimensions haven't changed.

---

# Why Does Reflow Matter?

A single reflow isn't usually a problem.

The problem is performing **large numbers of layout-changing operations repeatedly**.

For example:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {

    const box = document.createElement("div");

    box.textContent = i;

    document.body.appendChild(box);
}
```

This repeatedly modifies the DOM.

Depending on the situation, the browser may have to perform a lot of rendering work.

For large applications, inefficient DOM manipulation can contribute to poor performance.

---

# Bad Pattern

Imagine:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {

    element.style.width = i + "px";
}
```

You're changing the layout repeatedly.

Conceptually:

```text
Change width
 ↓
Layout work

Change width
 ↓
Layout work

Change width
 ↓
Layout work

...
```

This can be inefficient.

---

# Better Approach

Instead of repeatedly changing the DOM or styles, make changes in batches where possible.

For example:

```javascript
element.style.width = "100px";
element.style.height = "100px";
element.style.margin = "20px";
```

Or use a CSS class:

```css
.large-box {
    width: 100px;
    height: 100px;
    margin: 20px;
}
```

Then:

```javascript
element.classList.add("large-box");
```

This keeps styling in CSS and can make DOM updates easier to manage.

---

# Using `classList`

Instead of:

```javascript
element.style.width = "300px";
element.style.height = "200px";
element.style.backgroundColor = "black";
element.style.color = "white";
```

You can define:

```css
.active {
    width: 300px;
    height: 200px;
    background: black;
    color: white;
}
```

Then:

```javascript
element.classList.add("active");
```

This is generally cleaner and easier to maintain.

---

# DocumentFragment

When creating many DOM elements, `DocumentFragment` can be useful.

Example:

```javascript
const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {

    const item = document.createElement("div");

    item.textContent = "Item " + i;

    fragment.appendChild(item);
}

document.body.appendChild(fragment);
```

The elements are built inside the fragment first.

Then the fragment is added to the document.

This can reduce unnecessary intermediate DOM updates.

